---
// src/pages/projects/seo-intelligence-bot.astro
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import SignalFeed from '../components/SignalFeed.jsx';

const title = 'SEO Intelligence Bot — Cuemio';
const description = 'A modular signal engine that turns crypto news, market anomalies, and search trends into ranked, AI-interpreted content angles delivered to Telegram.';
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} theme="light">
	<div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 md:px-10 py-20 font-body">

		<!-- Back link -->
		<a href="/#projects" class="font-plex-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-[#565C63] hover:text-[#12151A] transition-colors inline-flex items-center gap-1 mb-8">
			&larr; Back to modules
		</a>

		<!-- Header -->
		<div class="mb-4">
			<span class="cue-light is-go font-plex-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-widest text-[#565C63]">
				<span class="dot"></span>
				Live
			</span>
		</div>
		<h1 class="font-display text-4xl md:text-5xl font-semibold text-[#12151A] mb-4">
			SEO Intelligence Bot
		</h1>
		<p class="text-lg text-[#565C63] mb-12 max-w-2xl leading-relaxed">
			The unfair advantage in SEO isn't writing faster than everyone else &mdash;
			it's knowing what to write before the opportunity is gone. This is the
			system I built to know first.
		</p>

		<!-- Tech stack -->
		<div class="flex flex-wrap gap-2 mb-16">
			{['Python', 'OpenAI API', 'Telegram API', 'Railway', 'Search Console', 'RSS', 'n8n'].map((tech) => (
				<span class="text-xs px-3 py-1.5 bg-white text-[#565C63] rounded-md border border-[#DEE2DC]">
					{tech}
				</span>
			))}
		</div>

		<!-- The problem -->
		<section class="mb-16">
			<h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-[#12151A] mb-4">The problem</h2>
			<p class="text-[#565C63] leading-relaxed mb-4">
				Most SEO content calendars are reactive by the time they're built.
				A trend surfaces, someone notices it in a Slack channel two days
				later, a brief gets written, and by the time the article ships the
				search opportunity has already peaked &mdash; or ten competitors
				beat you to it.
			</p>
			<p class="text-[#565C63] leading-relaxed">
				News alone isn't a strategy either. Everyone reading the same
				headlines produces the same content. The actual edge comes from
				layering signals that arrive <em class="text-[#12151A] not-italic font-medium">before</em>
				the obvious ones &mdash; market anomalies, rising search queries,
				your own ranking movements, competitor gaps &mdash; and having
				something fast enough to catch all of them at once.
			</p>
		</section>

		<!-- The system -->
		<section class="mb-16">
			<h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-[#12151A] mb-4">The system</h2>
			<p class="text-[#565C63] leading-relaxed mb-6">
				Seven signal sources run on independent schedules &mdash; from
				15-minute news sweeps to daily Search Console pulls. Every raw
				signal gets passed through an LLM interpretation layer before it
				ever reaches a human: the model decides whether it's noise, assigns
				a priority, and writes a specific content angle rather than just
				flagging that something happened.
			</p>
			<div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
				{[
					{ label: 'News & narrative', detail: 'RSS from major crypto outlets, scanned every 15 minutes' },
					{ label: 'Market anomalies', detail: 'Price, volume, and funding-rate spikes via CoinGecko / CoinGlass' },
					{ label: 'Search trends', detail: 'Rising queries before they show up in competitor content' },
					{ label: 'Own performance', detail: 'Search Console pulls flagging striking-distance rankings' },
					{ label: 'Competitor gaps', detail: 'What rival academy blogs just published, and what they missed' },
					{ label: 'Token unlocks', detail: 'Scheduled events with predictable market impact' },
				].map((item) => (
					<div class="p-4 bg-white border border-[#DEE2DC] rounded-xl">
						<p class="text-sm font-semibold text-[#12151A] mb-1">{item.label}</p>
						<p class="text-xs text-[#565C63] leading-relaxed">{item.detail}</p>
					</div>
				))}
			</div>
		</section>

		<!-- Live demo -->
		<section class="mb-16">
			<h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-[#12151A] mb-2">See it work</h2>
			<p class="text-[#565C63] text-sm mb-6">
				A simulated version of the live feed below &mdash; same format,
				same priority tiers, same interpretation-before-alert pipeline as
				what actually runs in production. Real output stays private since
				it reflects live competitive strategy.
			</p>
			<SignalFeed client:load />
		</section>

		<!-- What it demonstrates -->
		<section class="mb-16">
			<h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-[#12151A] mb-4">What this demonstrates</h2>
			<p class="text-[#565C63] leading-relaxed">
				This is the difference between doing SEO and building the system
				that does SEO for you. Seven independent data sources, a shared
				interpretation layer, deduplication so nothing repeats, and a
				delivery channel that meets me where I already am. It's the same
				instinct from materials engineering applied to content strategy:
				stop reacting to individual failures, build the system that
				catches them before they happen.
			</p>
		</section>

	</div>
</Layout>
